# Git Hygiene

## Purpose

Maintain clean Git history, safe staging, clear commits, and reliable push behavior.

## When This Applies

- Any code change
- Any commit
- Any push
- Any branch operation
- Any release preparation

## Required Workflow

1. Run git status before staging.
2. Inspect git diff before committing.
3. Stage only related files.
4. Use a conventional commit message.
5. Verify branch and remote before pushing.

## Standards

- Commit messages should explain the value of the change.
- Keep commits scoped to the task.
- Preserve user work that is already in progress.
- Never include secrets or local machine files.
- Report the branch and commit after pushing.

## Do Not

- Do not use git add . without reviewing the diff.
- Do not amend pushed commits unless explicitly approved.
- Do not force push protected branches.
- Do not commit .env, logs, credentials, or generated junk.
- Do not mix unrelated changes into one commit.

## Verification Checklist

- The existing project pattern was inspected before changes were made.
- The implementation is scoped to the user request.
- Unsafe files, secrets, and unrelated changes are excluded.
- Applicable lint, typecheck, test, build, or validation commands were run when available.
- Any skipped or failed verification is reported clearly.

## Related Skills

- Use the matching `.cursor/skills/` workflow when one exists for this rule.
- When no matching skill exists, follow this rule directly and keep changes minimal, safe, and verifiable.
